1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,328
JAMIE: Jamie Dimon just told the world JPMorgan has twenty billion dollars burning a hole in its pocket, and the stock dropped three point four two percent—because apparently Wall Street's love language is panic.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/27/jamie-dimon-jpmorgan-chase-acquisition-target-size.html

2
00:00:12,328 --> 00:01:03,883
ALEX: So Iran's internet came back online this morning, which sounds like good news until you realize oil markets are pricing in escalation anyway. The actual conflict could end tomorrow and we'd still be paying the anxiety tax. Markets are doing their best impression of a person who's fine, really fine, everything's fine. S&P down point one six percent, Nasdaq down point three four, but here's where it gets weird—Procter & Gamble is up three point three five percent, Coca-Cola up two point four one, PepsiCo up one point nine nine. Defensive stocks are rallying like the apocalypse is scheduled for Thursday. Meanwhile, JPMorgan is down three point four two percent, which tracks with Jamie's opening—Dimon's twenty billion dollar war chest announcement apparently spooked people. Nothing says "we have a plan" like losing market cap before lunch.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/27/jamie-dimon-jpmorgan-chase-acquisition-target-size.html

3
00:01:03,883 --> 00:01:17,706
JAMIE: Wait, so the market saw JPMorgan's massive cash position and thought, "Hmm, sounds like they're worried about something." That's the financial equivalent of your friend saying they just got a really good deal on a bunker.

4
00:01:17,706 --> 00:01:42,363
ALEX: Exactly. And it's not just JPMorgan. Semiconductors are getting demolished—A M D down two point eight eight, Nvidia down two point four seven. The V I X is actually falling, down point four seven, which is the deadpan part—volatility's calm but everything expensive is selling off. This is what happens when you have a market that's simultaneously decided "risk is over" and "growth is suspicious."

5
00:01:42,363 --> 00:01:56,186
MAYA: So hold on, hold on—Procter & Gamble up three point three five percent while Nvidia's bleeding? That's not a rotation, that's a full evacuation. People are literally running from the future and into consumer staples.

6
00:01:56,186 --> 00:02:15,986
ALEX: The dollar's barely moving, but crude's down. Oil's actually the only thing that makes sense today. Iran comes back online, traders price in peace, and W T I falls. Gold's down, Bitcoin down. Risk-off across the board, except the market's pretending everything's fine by making Coca-Cola the hottest stock of the morning.

7
00:02:15,986 --> 00:02:26,447
JAMIE: So what's the actual thesis here? Are we in a "soft landing confirmed" moment, or is this just people panic-hedging into staples because they think something's coming?

8
00:02:26,447 --> 00:02:49,236
MAYA: Okay, so there's a database company called ClickHouse that just tripled its annualized revenue to two hundred fifty million dollars, and literally nobody outside of infrastructure nerds knows who they are. Two hundred fifty million. That's not a side project—that's a real business that's apparently been printing money in the shadows while we've all been obsessed with AI chatbots.

9
00:02:49,236 --> 00:02:54,840
JAMIE: Wait, ClickHouse? I've genuinely never heard of this company. What do they actually do?

10
00:02:54,840 --> 00:03:11,278
MAYA: They make a database that's really fast at analyzing massive amounts of data. Think of it as an alternative to other data warehouse solutions—no venture capital theater, just a project that somehow convinced enterprises to pay them a quarter billion dollars a year.

11
00:03:11,278 --> 00:03:33,319
ALEX: A quarter billion dollars in A R R and we're on turn three of a podcast beat. That's the actual story here—this is what happens when you build something useful instead of something that requires a T E D talk to explain. This is the kind of enterprise software play that's weird, unglamorous, completely functional, and apparently unstoppable.

12
00:03:33,319 --> 00:03:43,033
JAMIE: So the market's obsessed with Nvidia and AI infrastructure plays, but the actual infrastructure that makes AI work—the databases—is just quietly becoming a monster business?

13
00:03:43,033 --> 00:03:56,108
MAYA: Exactly. And here's the absurd part—they're eyeing an IPO within the next few years, which means they're probably already at a significant valuation, and Wall Street's going to pretend they discovered this company yesterday.

14
00:03:56,108 --> 00:04:20,018
ALEX: The database market is genuinely massive—we're talking hundreds of billions in total addressable market—and it's one of the few enterprise software categories where you can actually have a profitable, growing business without needing to convince investors you're going to replace human consciousness. ClickHouse's two hundred fifty million A R R is probably doing better margins than most SaaS companies at twice the revenue.

15
00:04:20,018 --> 00:04:30,852
JAMIE: So when they do go public, what's the actual pitch? Is it "we're the boring, profitable alternative," or do they get caught up in the same hype cycle?

16
00:04:30,852 --> 00:04:56,630
MAYA: They're going to try to position themselves as the "open-source, community-driven, anti-establishment database play," which will last approximately four minutes into the investor roadshow before someone asks about AI capabilities and suddenly they're claiming their database is essential for various enterprise applications. This stock has commitment issues—it'll either be valued like a boring infrastructure company or hyped like the next big thing, and there's no in-between.

17
00:04:56,630 --> 00:05:38,472
ALEX: The real tell is going to be the IPO price. If they price it at a reasonable multiple to revenue, then we know the market's actually functioning. If it's much higher and they're calling themselves "the AI database," then we're back to a situation where nobody understands what's happening, but the momentum's real, so we're all just along for the ride. Abercrombie & Fitch is up thirteen percent on an earnings beat, which would be great except sales in certain regions dropped because of the Iran conflict. So the market saw "we crushed expectations" and completely ignored "our actual business is shrinking in affected areas." This stock has commitment issues.

18
00:05:38,472 --> 00:05:45,197
JAMIE: Wait, so they beat on earnings but revenue's contracting in some regions? How does that math work?

19
00:05:45,197 --> 00:05:59,020
ALEX: Cost-cutting and margin expansion in the regions that still exist. It's the financial equivalent of saying your restaurant's doing great because you fired half the staff and stopped buying fresh ingredients. The headline won the day.

20
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:16,952
MAYA: Okay, but Foot Locker's actually back to growth, right? Dick's Sporting Goods acquired them and now Dick's is down because the turnaround's expensive. That's not a bifurcation, that's just Dick's chose violence today by reporting weak numbers while their own acquisition is doing the heavy lifting.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/27/dicks-sporting-goods-dks-earnings-q1-2026.html

21
00:06:16,952 --> 00:06:35,258
ALEX: Dick's sales surged post-acquisition but profitability got demolished because Foot Locker's turnaround costs money. So Dick's is basically saying, "We bought growth and it's destroying our margins." Foot Locker's up, Dick's is down, and the sneaker market's apparently figured out it wants one of them but not both.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/27/dicks-sporting-goods-dks-earnings-q1-2026.html

22
00:06:35,258 --> 00:06:42,730
JAMIE: So the market's like, "I respect the acquisition strategy, but I'm going to punish you for actually executing it."

23
00:06:42,730 --> 00:07:03,277
MAYA: Okay, Iran's internet shutdown just ended, and Iranians are describing it like being released from prison. That's not just a connectivity story—that's a freedom story. The government cut them off during the conflict, and now they're back online and everyone's just trying to catch up on what the world did without them.

24
00:07:03,277 --> 00:07:20,462
ALEX: The New York Fed just reported a remarkable increase in food insecurity, which is Fed-speak for "we're genuinely shocked this is happening while the stock market's at all-time highs." The S&P's ripping, Coca-Cola's rallying, and apparently millions of Americans are skipping meals. That tracks.

25
00:07:20,462 --> 00:07:24,572
JAMIE: What's the actual number on food insecurity from the Fed?

26
00:07:24,572 --> 00:07:35,406
ALEX: They reported a remarkable increase, but the specific percentage wasn't detailed in the initial report. When the New York Fed says something's remarkable, it means it's actually remarkable.

27
00:07:35,406 --> 00:07:57,074
MAYA: Okay, Ferrari just launched a new all-electric vehicle, and the Ferrari fan community is absolutely dunking on it. Like, we're talking highly upvoted Reddit threads comparing it to other vehicles. One commenter said it "looks like what happens when you ask ChatGPT to design a sports car," and that's the nicest thing anyone's said about it.

28
00:07:57,074 --> 00:08:07,535
JAMIE: Wait, Ferrari's first E V looks like other vehicles? How does that even happen? They've had a hundred years to figure out what a Ferrari looks like.

29
00:08:07,535 --> 00:08:32,565
ALEX: The vehicle is an all-electric model that looks like someone told Ferrari's design team, "Make it practical," and they responded by removing every distinguishing feature the brand's ever had. It's the automotive equivalent of a startup that raised hundreds of millions of dollars to solve a problem nobody had—except in this case, the problem was "how do we make a Ferrari that nobody wants to drive?"
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/transportation/938016/this-ferrari-should-have-been-a-volkswagen

30
00:08:32,565 --> 00:08:53,860
MAYA: The worst part is Ferrari's trying to position this as the future of the brand, which means in five years they're going to have multiple electric models in every color, and actual Ferrari enthusiasts are just going to start buying used models from decades past because at least those looked like something worth the price.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/transportation/938016/this-ferrari-should-have-been-a-volkswagen

31
00:08:53,860 --> 00:09:29,724
ALEX: JPMorgan's twenty billion dollar acquisition budget is the financial equivalent of a billionaire saying they're going to the gym—Jamie Dimon's been talking about buying regional banks and fintech platforms for years, and the only thing he's actually closed is the Wilmington Trust deal, which was five billion and felt like a warm-up lap. If he actually deploys twenty billion, it'll be the most aggressive M&A spree in banking since 2008, but odds are we're watching banker theater where the real play is keeping the cash on the balance sheet and pretending optionality is a strategy.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/27/jamie-dimon-jpmorgan-chase-acquisition-target-size.html

32
00:09:29,724 --> 00:09:53,261
MAYA: The thing that kills me is JPMorgan's already the biggest bank in America—like, they don't need to buy anything, they're just doing this because Dimon's bored and has a competitive complex about being smaller than his own institution's ambitions. It's the corporate equivalent of someone who's already won the game deciding to play on hard mode just to feel something.

33
00:09:53,261 --> 00:09:58,491
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
